  "step1_summary": "- Chief complaint: Six weeks of generalized aching and fatigue (tiredness), with a recent episode of reduced appetite; patient notes feeling run-down and less energetic than usual.\n- Symptoms (onset/duration/severity/aggravating factors): Onset around late January to February after returning from holiday in the States; persistent over ~6 weeks; fatigue with decreased exercise tolerance; occasional decreased appetite; intermittent sore throat and a brief fever around the time of a transient skin rash on the back of the calf that resolved within a couple of weeks; no cough, dyspnea, chest symptoms, or persistent GI symptoms; no significant weight loss but appetite reduced; joint/muscle aches (shoulders, back, hips, knees) without swelling; concentration affecting work modestly.\n- Past medical history: Asthma and hay fever; uses inhaled “preventer”/daily inhaler inconsistently; takes antihistamines as needed.\n- Medications/allergies: Antihistamines; inhaled preventive inhaler (not consistently used); no known drug allergies mentioned.\n- Family/social history: Lives with wife; works for a health technology company; non-smoker; occasional alcohol; no other social risk factors discussed.\n- Vital signs/exam findings: Not available from transcript.\n- Labs/imaging (if mentioned): Doctor suggested blood tests (liver, kidney function, full blood count) and Lyme serology; no imaging results mentioned.\n- Other relevant clues: A transient skin rash on the calf occurred around late February and then faded; possible tick exposure suspected; holiday exposure to the US with outdoor activity; ongoing fatigue without clear focal neurologic or respiratory symptoms.\n",
